{:edb/db-before-t 1559 :edb/db-after-t 1560 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534872 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:43:14.878Z" 13194139534872 true] [17592186068508 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int  \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534872 true] [17592186068508 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534872 false] [17592186068508 1145 "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5x}{x^{2} + 3x - 4} &= (5x)/((x - 1)(x + 4)) \\\\\n&= (A)/((x - 1)) + (B)/((x + 4))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x &= A(x + 4) + B(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4$, we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(-4) &= A(-4 + 4) + B(-4 - 1) \\\\\n- 20 &= -5B \\\\\nB &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1$, we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5(1) &= A(1 + 4) + B(1 - 1) \\\\\n5 &= 5A \\\\\nA &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{5x}{x^{2} + 3x - 4}dx &= ∫(\\frac{1}{x - 1} + \\frac{4}{x + 4})dx \\\\\n&= ∫\\frac{1}{x - 1}dx + 4∫\\frac{1}{x + 4}dx \\\\\n&= \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x - 1 & + 4\\ln & x + 4\\end{vmatrix}+ C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534872 false] [17592186068508 1145 "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} & =\\frac{5x}{(x-1)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =A(x+4)+B(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4) & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-1) \\\\ -20 & =-5B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1) & =A(1+4)+B(1-1) \\\\ 5 & =5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4}  = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{4}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534872 true] [17592186068510 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534872 false] [17592186068510 1100 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534872 true] [17592186068510 1145 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2x + 3}{4x^{2} - 1} &= (2x + 3)/((2x + 1)(2x - 1)) \\\\\n&= (A)/((2x + 1)) + (B)/((2x - 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 3 &= A(2x - 1) + B(2x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(\\frac{1}{2}) + 3 &= A(2(\\frac{1}{2}) - 1) + B(2(\\frac{1}{2}) + 1) \\\\\n4 &= 2B \\\\\nB &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(-\\frac{1}{2}) + 3 &= A(2(-\\frac{1}{2}) - 1) + B(2(-\\frac{1}{2}) + 1) \\\\\n2 &= -2A \\\\\nA &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{2x + 3}{4x^{2} - 1}dx &= ∫(- (1)/((2x + 1)) + (2)/((2x - 1)))dx \\\\\n&= - ∫\\frac{1}{2x + 1}dx + 2∫\\frac{1}{2x - 1}dx \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}2x + 1 & + 2(\\frac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid 2x - 1 \\mid) + C \\\\ =-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln & 2x + 1\\end{vmatrix}+ \\ln \\mid 2x - 1 \\mid + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{2x + 3}{4x^{2} - 1}dx &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}2x + 1 & + \\ln & 2x - 1 & + C \\\\ = \\ln & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x + 1}} & + \\ln & 2x - 1\\end{vmatrix}+ C \\\\\n&= \\ln \\mid \\frac{2x - 1}{\\sqrt{2x + 1}} \\mid + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x + 3}{4x^{2} - 1}dx &= \\ln \\mid \\frac{2x - 1}{\\sqrt{2x + 1}} \\mid _{1}^{4} \\\\\n&= \\ln (\\frac{7}{3}) - \\ln (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (\\frac{7}{3}) + \\ln (\\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534872 false] [17592186068510 1145 "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534872 true] [17592186068847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316386 13194139534872 true] [17592186068847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316393 13194139534872 true] [17592186068847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316400 13194139534872 true] [17592186068847 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316418 13194139534872 true] [17592186068848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316407 13194139534872 true] [17592186068848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316411 13194139534872 true] [17592186068848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316414 13194139534872 true] [17592186068848 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316425 13194139534872 true] [17592186069300 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534872 true] [17592186069300 110